<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Lima (1929)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, signed on 3 June 1929 with the mediation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> dispute left by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>. Tacna was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and Arica remained with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, which paid Peru compensation and agreed to provide port facilities. The settlement ended nearly half a century of tension between the two countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lima-1929.jpg" alt="Treaty of Lima (1929)" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Janitoalevic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tacna-Arica_Dispute_(1883-1929).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the War of the Pacific Chile defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and conquered the Peruvian territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ancon">Treaty of Ancón</a> of 1883 Tarapacá was annexed to Chile, while Tacna and Arica stayed under Chilean administration until a plebiscite due in 1893. The plebiscite never took place, as the two countries could not agree on its terms. Chile began a campaign of Chilenization in 1909, and in 1911 Peru recalled its ambassador and broke off diplomatic relations.</p><p>On 20 July 1922 the two countries agreed to submit the dispute to the arbitration of the President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. President Calvin Coolidge appointed General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-j-pershing">John J. Pershing</a> as arbitrator in 1925, and General William Lassiter took over in 1926 after Pershing resigned on 18 January, but neither could break the deadlock. In 1928 the Secretary of State, Frank B. Kellogg, suggested direct negotiations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, which led to the treaty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> by the Peruvian representative Pedro José Rada y Gamio and the Chilean representative Emiliano Figueroa Larrain. It divided Tacna Province, the administrative division <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> had established, in two: Tacna went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and Chile kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a>. It fixed the border between the two states, Chile agreed to pay Peru up to US$6 million in compensation, and Peru received the administration of the Tacna–Arica railway and a Peruvian-administered wharf in the port of Arica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>'s ruling elite, which had hoped to gain access to the sea, saw the compromise as a setback, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> had promoted an option giving Bolivia such access. The treaty stiffened Bolivian positions on the Chaco and contributed to the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> in 1932. Only in 1999 did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> agree to implement the treaty fully, providing Peru with its port facilities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Lima (1929)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the Pacific reshaped the map of South America, leaving Bolivia without a coastline, a loss Bolivia has never accepted, and giving Chile the provinces of Tarapacá and Antofagasta, while the dispute over Tacna and Arica was settled with Peru only by the <b>Treaty of Lima</b> of 1929.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
